# Derived by hand from Semantics ch. 2, Regular expressions, and Appendix B: each example's match spans by leftmost-first search (`yes` takes `yes` after `y` fails at `$`; `host` stops at the first `/` after the host); a literal prints as the plain form, a bare `/` written `\/` (Type System ch. 2, RX-EQ); after the closing slash a terminator makes the letters flags, a `/` makes them a letters-only replacement (`%/a//` deletes), any other character after no letters begins a replacement, and in the hashed form letters before a space begin one that runs to the next `/#`.
